Subject: [PATCH] rfp: the per-site audio noise on what a node reads inside an offline rendering

Patch 0012 puts per-site noise (RFPTarget::AudioRandomization) on the buffer
an OfflineAudioContext returns. A node inside the offline graph hands values
to script while the rendering runs, before that noise, and they are as
steady as the returned buffer: the same on every site. This patch gives them
the same noise, in the five places where they leave the graph:

1. An AnalyserNode's four getters. They are all made from one copy of the
   last samples (GetTimeDomainData), and that copy gets the noise.
2. A ScriptProcessorNode's input buffer, as the audioprocess event is made.
3. An AudioWorklet's inputs, as they are copied into the arrays process()
   reads, on the rendering thread.
4. The values of a worklet's parameter that a node is connected to, which
   carry that node's samples.
5. A DynamicsCompressorNode's reduction, a number the compressor makes of
   the samples.

In each place the noise goes on the copy that script reads. What the graph
holds and passes on is not changed.

The noise is the one patch 0012 added: each value moves by at most one part
in ten thousand, by a step of its own. It comes from the site's key (random
per site and per session, the canvas noise's) and from the values
themselves, so two sites read different values and one site reads the same
values again for the same sound. The worklet's part runs on the rendering
thread, where the document and its cookie jar settings may not be used. So
the context copies the site's key once, when it is made, and
RandomizeAudioSamplesWithKey makes the seed from that copy with no object
of the main thread, no pref and no allocation (the seed the engine's canvas
noise makes for a small image).

Only an offline context has a key. A context that plays is not changed:
a script processor or a worklet can pass what it read on to the speakers
or into a call, and what a person hears is not touched (ADR 0019).
The byte getters of an analyser round to 256 steps, which hides most of the
noise, and most of what differs between two computers with it.

No new target and no pref: the readers follow AudioRandomization, which
Nulavio already turns on, with the engine's exemptions per site.

Owner: Nulavio maintainers
Upstream: not proposed. Mozilla's own audio protection is tracked in its
bug 1358149.
